Error [jet.ReadOnlyValueNotOwned]: `hs` was not moved here, so this loop can't take its handles
  --> tests/ui/loop_two_binding_borrowed_task_list.jet:3:20
    |
  3 |     loop (i, h) in hs -> total += (h.join() ?? 0) + i
    |                    ^^
 Why: Each step hands you the handle itself, and a task handle cannot be copied — so the loop takes the whole list
 Fix: Take the list: `hs: &&[Task<Int>]`, or collect the work in a canonical `task.all` or `task.group` block
More: jet-lang.dev/e/E0120

Error [jet.SingleUseLent]: `handles` can't be shared — it must be used exactly once
  --> tests/ui/loop_two_binding_borrowed_task_list.jet:9:17
    |
  9 |     print(drain(handles))
    |                 ^^^^^^^
 Why: This value's type is `#SingleUse`, so it can only be moved (handed over for good); `drain` would borrow or copy it, and a `#SingleUse` value has no second use to give
 Fix: Give it away with `handles.move()`, or rework `drain` so it takes ownership with a `&&` parameter
More: jet-lang.dev/e/E0142

Error [jet.SingleUseDropped]: `handles` still owes `consume`
  --> tests/ui/loop_two_binding_borrowed_task_list.jet:8:5
    |
  8 |     handles: [task 1, task 2]
    |     ^^^^^^^
 Why: This value's type is `#SingleUse`, so it carries a job that has to be done — dropping it without doing that job leaves the work undone (an unjoined task, an unreleased lock)
 Fix: Consume it exactly once: pass it to a `&&` parameter, or `return` it — or write `#Unsafe("reason") { consume(handles) }` to discard it deliberately
More: jet-lang.dev/e/E0140
